--TEST--
Spec § 18's `serverVersion()` answers what this connection's handshake already delivered: a version that is there, kept on the connection rather than derived, and the same string on every call — `rule:core-classes/db-one-api` and ADR 0187 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: Core\Db\Connection::serverVersion
// SQLite is the backend a case can reach, and it is also the one with no
// server: its answer is the version of the library this binary is linked
// against, where the four wire drivers answer what their own handshake sent.
// All five are asserted against real servers from
// `crates/nvs-stdlib/tests/db_stream.rs`, which is the only place a greeting
// or a `LOGINACK` exists to be compared against.
var $db = Core\Db::connect("main");
var $version = $db->serverVersion();

// The member never answers nothing. Every one of the five backends has a
// version it already said, so there is no connection this is empty on and no
// `null` in the signature for one.
echo "answers: ", $version == "" ? "nothing" : "a version", "\n";

// It is *kept*, not asked for: the string is the one the connection has been
// holding since it opened, so a second call cannot answer differently. A
// member that ran `select sqlite_version()` here would be free to.
echo "twice: ", $version == $db->serverVersion() ? "the same" : "something else", "\n";

// And running statements does not move it. The version belongs to the server
// on the other end rather than to anything this request did to it.
$db->execute("create table t (id integer primary key)", []);
echo "after a statement: ", $version == $db->serverVersion() ? "the same" : "something else", "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# § 3: `db.connect` covers a SQLite block, whose path is configuration.
[capabilities.db]
connect = ["main"]

[db.main]
driver = "sqlite"
path = ":memory:"
--EXPECT--
answers: a version
twice: the same
after a statement: the same
